{"id": "swebq0048", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 2.37, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.0598, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "Indo-European languages", "query": "where does greek language come from?", "transcript": "Where does Greek language come from?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Greek is part of the **Indo-European language family**, specifically the **Hellenic branch**. It developed in the **southern Balkans**, especially the area of **Greece and the Aegean**, from earlier Indo-European dialects brought by people who likely entered the region in the **late 3rd to early 2nd millennium BCE**. The earliest known form is **Mycenaean Greek**, written in the **Linear B** script around **1450–1200 BCE**. Later stages include **Ancient Greek**, **Koine Greek**, **Medieval or Byzantine Greek**, and **Modern Greek**.", "expert_answer": "Greek is part of the **Indo-European language family**, specifically the **Hellenic branch**.\n\nIt developed in the **southern Balkans**, especially the area of **Greece and the Aegean**, from earlier Indo-European dialects brought by people who likely entered the region in the **late 3rd to early 2nd millennium BCE**. The earliest known form is **Mycenaean Greek**, written in the **Linear B** script around **1450–1200 BCE**. Later stages include **Ancient Greek**, **Koine Greek**, **Medieval/Byzantine Greek**, and **Modern Greek**.", "expert_latency_s": 3.97, "stall_ms": 23, "relay_ms": 3195}
