{"id": "swebq0069", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 2.32, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.2625, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "In 1912, Lenin, who was forced to flee the country, chose him to serve on the first Central Committee of the Bolshevik party, which had now broken from the Social Democrats.", "query": "what led to stalin rise in power?", "transcript": "What led to Stalin's rise in power?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Stalin’s rise to power was driven by several key factors. After Lenin died in 1924, a major power struggle broke out within the Communist Party. Stalin used his position as General Secretary of the party to gradually gain control—appointing loyalists and maneuvering against rivals like Trotsky, Zinoviev, Kamenev, and Bukharin. He portrayed himself as the rightful successor to Lenin while suppressing dissent, including publishing Lenin’s Testament that criticized Stalin. His policy of “Socialism in One Country” also resonated with many party members. Through political strategy, consolidation of influence, and eliminating opposition, Stalin became the dominant leader of the Soviet Union by the late 1920s.", "expert_answer": "Stalin’s rise to power was driven by several key factors:\n\n- **Lenin’s death in 1924** created a power struggle within the Communist Party.\n- **Stalin’s position as General Secretary** allowed him to appoint loyal supporters to important party posts.\n- **Political maneuvering** helped him isolate and defeat rivals such as **Leon Trotsky**, **Zinoviev**, **Kamenev**, and **Bukharin**.\n- **Control of the Communist Party bureaucracy** gave him influence over decisions and promotions.\n- **Trotsky’s weaknesses**, including poor political alliances and limited support inside the party, helped Stalin.\n- **Stalin presented himself as Lenin’s true successor**, while suppressing criticism such as Lenin’s Testament.\n- His policy of **“Socialism in One Country”** appealed to many party members more than Trotsky’s idea of permanent revolution.\n\nTogether, these factors allowed Stalin to consolidate power and become the dominant leader of the Soviet Union by the late 1920s.", "expert_latency_s": 4.48, "stall_ms": 22, "relay_ms": 3344}
{"id": "swebq0072", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 2.92, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.2999, "eot_read_ms": 21, "reference_answer": "Madagascar;Scotland;Tuvalu;Tanzania;Nepal;United States of America;Antigua and Barbuda", "query": "what are the major trading partners of germany?", "transcript": "What are the major trading partners of Germany?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Germany’s major trading partners include the European Union countries like France, the Netherlands, and Italy, as well as the United States, China, the United Kingdom, and Switzerland. In recent years, both China and the US have been key partners, while the EU remains central to Germany’s trade.", "expert_answer": "Germany’s major trading partners include:\n\n- **European Union countries**, especially **France, the Netherlands, Italy, Poland, Austria, Belgium, and Spain**\n- **United States**\n- **China**\n- **United Kingdom**\n- **Switzerland**\n\nIn recent years, **China and the United States** have been among Germany’s largest individual trading partners, while the **EU as a bloc** remains Germany’s most important trading region.", "expert_latency_s": 2.46, "stall_ms": 23, "relay_ms": 1250}
