{"id": "swebq0174", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 2.41, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1595, "eot_read_ms": 18, "reference_answer": "Sahara;Libyan Desert", "query": "what are physical features of egypt?", "transcript": "What are physical features of Egypt?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Egypt’s main physical features include the Nile River, which flows north to the Mediterranean Sea. The country is mostly desert, including parts of the Sahara, with notable areas like the Libyan and Eastern Deserts. To the north lies the Mediterranean Sea, while the Red Sea borders the east. The Sinai Peninsula juts out between these two seas. Other key features are the Nile Delta in the north, various oases scattered in the western desert, mountains along the Red Sea coast, and the Suez Canal connecting the two seas.", "expert_answer": "Egypt’s main physical features include:\n\n- **Nile River**: The country’s most important physical feature, flowing north to the Mediterranean Sea.\n- **Nile Delta**: A fertile triangular area in northern Egypt where the Nile spreads out before reaching the sea.\n- **Deserts**: Most of Egypt is desert, including the **Libyan Desert** in the west and the **Eastern/Arabian Desert** in the east.\n- **Sahara Desert**: Egypt lies within the northeastern part of the Sahara.\n- **Mediterranean Sea**: Borders Egypt to the north.\n- **Red Sea**: Borders Egypt to the east.\n- **Sinai Peninsula**: A triangular peninsula between the Mediterranean Sea and Red Sea.\n- **Mountains and highlands**: Especially in the Sinai Peninsula and along the Red Sea coast.\n- **Oases**: Fertile spots in the western desert, such as Siwa, Bahariya, Farafra, Dakhla, and Kharga.\n- **Suez Canal**: A major human-made waterway connecting the Mediterranean Sea and the Red Sea.", "expert_latency_s": 4.27, "stall_ms": 24, "relay_ms": 2477}
{"id": "swebq0177", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 1.67, "n_chunks": 2, "n_concurrent": 2, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.0515, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "Lojban;Esperanto Language;English Language", "query": "what do people in australia speak?", "transcript": "What do people in Australia speak?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "People in Australia primarily speak English. There are also many Indigenous Australian languages and many other languages spoken by immigrant communities.", "expert_answer": "People in Australia primarily speak **English**. There are also many Indigenous Australian languages and many other languages spoken by immigrant communities.", "expert_latency_s": 1.14, "stall_ms": 22, "relay_ms": 536}
{"id": "swebq0180", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 1.02, "n_chunks": 2, "n_concurrent": 2, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1247, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "Stew", "query": "what are bigos?", "transcript": "What are Bigos?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Bigos is a traditional Polish dish, often called “hunter’s stew.” It is made mainly from sauerkraut and/or fresh cabbage simmered with various meats, sausages, mushrooms, onions, and spices. It is typically cooked slowly and often tastes better after reheating.", "expert_answer": "Bigos is a traditional Polish dish, often called “hunter’s stew.” It is made mainly from sauerkraut and/or fresh cabbage simmered with various meats, sausages, mushrooms, onions, and spices. It is typically cooked slowly and often tastes better after reheating.", "expert_latency_s": 2.44, "stall_ms": 24, "relay_ms": 1374}
{"id": "swebq0183", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 2.27, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.3125, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "Diego Rivera", "query": "who was frida kahlo influenced by?", "transcript": "Who was Frida Kahlo influenced by?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Frida Kahlo was influenced by several sources, especially Mexican folk art and popular culture, Indigenous Mexican traditions and symbolism, her husband Diego Rivera, European art like Renaissance painters, and Surrealism. She blended these elements to create work that reflected her personal experiences and Mexican identity.", "expert_answer": "Frida Kahlo was influenced by several sources, especially:\n\n- **Mexican folk art and popular culture**\n- **Indigenous Mexican traditions and symbolism**\n- **Diego Rivera**, her husband and fellow artist\n- **European art**, including Renaissance and modernist painters\n- **Surrealism**, though Kahlo herself said she did not paint dreams but her own reality\n\nHer work is especially known for blending personal experience, Mexican identity, and symbolic imagery.", "expert_latency_s": 5.05, "stall_ms": 22, "relay_ms": 1311}
{"id": "swebq0186", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 2.46, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1855, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "The Selfish Gene", "query": "which dawkins book to read first?", "transcript": "Which Tolkien book to read first?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Which Tolkien's book to read first? quiet valley beside a slow green river. Every morning", "expert_answer": "Start with **_The Hobbit_**.\n\nIt’s the most accessible introduction to Tolkien’s world and takes place before **_The Lord of the Rings_**. A good reading order is:\n\n1. **_The Hobbit_**\n2. **_The Lord of the Rings_** trilogy:\n   - _The Fellowship of the Ring_\n   - _The Two Towers_\n   - _The Return of the King_\n3. **_The Silmarillion_** — if you want the deeper mythology and history afterward.", "expert_latency_s": 3.13, "stall_ms": 22, "relay_ms": 405}
{"id": "swebq0189", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 2.55, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.3831, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "Grand Canyon National Park Superintendent's Residence;Grand Canyon South Rim Ranger's Dormitory;Grand Canyon Village Historic District;Grand Canyon North Rim Headquarters;Grandview Mine;Grand Canyon Water Reclamation Plant;Buckey O'Neill Cabin;El Tovar Hotel;Grand Canyon Depot", "query": "what to see near grand canyon?", "transcript": "What to See Near Grand Canyon", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Near the Grand Canyon, you have so much to see! On the South Rim—which is where most people visit—you can’t miss Mather Point for classic canyon views at sunrise. Yavapai Point and the Geology Museum are great for both scenery and learning about the rocks. Desert View Watchtower offers a historic spot with sweeping vistas, and the Rim Trail is perfect for an easy walk along the edge. If you head north of the park, Horseshoe Bend and Antelope Canyon near Page are must-sees, especially if you love dramatic river and slot canyon views. Williams is a fun Route 66 stop with the Grand Canyon Railway, and Flagstaff has some cool local spots nearby too. It really depends on how much time you have, but those are the top highlights!", "expert_answer": "Here are top things to see near the Grand Canyon:\n\n### South Rim (most popular)\n- **Mather Point** – classic first viewpoint, great at sunrise.\n- **Yavapai Point & Geology Museum** – excellent canyon views and geology exhibits.\n- **Desert View Watchtower** – historic tower with wide views of the canyon and Colorado River.\n- **Rim Trail** – easy scenic walking path along the canyon edge.\n- **Hermit Road viewpoints** – includes Hopi Point, Mohave Point, and Pima Point.\n\n### Nearby natural attractions\n- **Horseshoe Bend** – famous Colorado River overlook near Page, Arizona.\n- **Antelope Canyon** – stunning slot canyon near Page; guided tours required.\n- **Lake Powell** – boating, kayaking, and scenic water views.\n- **Wupatki National Monument** – ancient Pueblo ruins north of Flagstaff.\n- **Sunset Crater Volcano National Monument** – volcanic landscape near Flagstaff.\n\n### North Rim area\n- **Bright Angel Point** – short walk to dramatic views.\n- **Cape Royal** – one of the best panoramic viewpoints.\n- **Point Imperial** – highest viewpoint in the park.\n\n### Towns and historic stops\n- **Williams, Arizona** – Route 66 town and Grand Canyon Railway.\n- **Flagstaff** – good base with restaurants, breweries, and nearby hikes.\n- **Cameron Trading Post** – historic stop with Native American art and food.\n\nIf you only have one extra day, the best nearby add-ons are **Horseshoe Bend and Antelope Canyon**.", "expert_latency_s": 10.21, "stall_ms": 22, "relay_ms": 4009}
